\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Schematic of a THz time-domain spectroscopy (THz-TDS) system. A femtosecond laser drives a photoconductive antenna (PCA) emitter and gates a PCA receiver. The time-delayed probe beam samples the THz electric field $E(t)$ point by point, yielding the full waveform including phase information.}}{2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:tds}{{1}{2}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Operating principle of a photoconductive antenna (PCA). A femtosecond optical pulse generates photocarriers in the electrode gap, which are accelerated by the bias voltage $V_{\mathrm  {bias}}$. The resulting transient current radiates a broadband THz pulse. The radiated field is proportional to $\partial J/\partial t$. For near-field applications, the electrode gap is reduced to subwavelength dimensions (1--5~$\mu $m).}}{2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:pca}{{2}{2}{}{}{}}
